=== L1 Describing Systems: Block Diagrams, Schematics and Flowcharts ===
id: a8cd94c8-a160-4346-aed9-1d6bb63df974
slug: describing-systems-block-diagrams-schematics-and-flowcharts
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:45:05.563163+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 31

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How engineers use block diagrams, schematics and flowcharts to describe systems.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Thinking in Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every engineered product &mdash; from a car braking system to a factory conveyor &mdash; is a <dfn class="term" data-def="An organised set of parts or sub-systems that work together to perform a function.">system</dfn>. Engineers break complicated products down into smaller, manageable pieces so they can design, build and test each part separately. The three main tools for doing this are <strong>block diagrams</strong>, <strong>schematic drawings</strong> and <strong>flowcharts</strong>. Understanding when to use each one is essential.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the three-box IPO model from memory, then give one real example for each box from a car engine.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The input&ndash;process&ndash;output (IPO) model is the foundation of all system design. Every system takes something in, does something to it, and produces a result. Identifying these three stages is the first step when analysing or designing any engineering system.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Input&ndash;Process&ndash;Output Model</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A model that describes a system in terms of what enters it (input), what it does (process), and what it produces (output).">input&ndash;process&ndash;output model</dfn> (IPO) lets engineers describe any system with three boxes. In an electric kettle: the input is the mains electricity and cold water; the process is the heating element converting electrical energy to heat; the output is boiling water. In a car engine: the input is fuel and air; the process is combustion; the output is rotary motion at the crankshaft.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">A system also has a <dfn class="term" data-def="The boundary drawn around a system that shows what is inside (part of the system) and what is outside (the environment).">system boundary</dfn> &mdash; an imaginary line that separates what belongs to the system from the wider environment. Drawing the boundary forces the engineer to decide exactly what is and is not part of their design.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">System Block Diagrams</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A diagram that uses labelled rectangles (blocks) connected by arrows to show how sub-systems are linked and how signals or energy flow between them.">system block diagram</dfn> uses rectangular blocks joined by arrows to show how sub-systems connect. Each block is labelled with what it does (e.g. &ldquo;temperature sensor&rdquo;, &ldquo;microcontroller&rdquo;, &ldquo;motor driver&rdquo;). The arrows show the direction of signal or energy flow.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Block diagrams are NOT circuit diagrams and NOT flowcharts. A circuit diagram shows how components are wired using standard symbols (resistor, capacitor, LED). A flowchart shows the sequence of steps a process or program follows. The examiner has repeatedly noted that students draw circuit diagrams when asked for block diagrams &mdash; these answers score zero.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Close this and sketch a block diagram for a burglar alarm from memory, labelling the input, process and output sub-systems.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A system block diagram uses labelled rectangles and arrows only &mdash; no component symbols (resistor zigzag, capacitor plates, logic gate shapes). If the diagram looks like a circuit, it is wrong. Blocks describe WHAT each sub-system does, not HOW it is built.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Open-Loop and Closed-Loop Control</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">In an <dfn class="term" data-def="A control system with no feedback path — the output has no influence on the input or process.">open-loop system</dfn> the output has no effect on the process. A toaster set to a timer is open-loop: it heats for a fixed time regardless of whether the toast is burnt or pale. In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A control system with a feedback path that compares the actual output to the desired output and adjusts the process accordingly.">closed-loop system</dfn> a sensor monitors the output and feeds information back to the controller. An oven thermostat is closed-loop: it measures actual temperature and switches the heating element on or off to match the set point.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Closed-loop systems are more accurate but more complex to design. Most industrial control systems &mdash; from a car cruise-control to a furnace temperature controller &mdash; are closed-loop because small disturbances (draughts, varying load) would cause the output to drift without feedback correction.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Sub-Systems and System Boundaries</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Large systems are broken into <dfn class="term" data-def="A smaller, self-contained system that forms part of a larger system and carries out a specific function.">sub-systems</dfn>. A car has sub-systems for engine management, braking, steering, lighting, infotainment and climate control. Each sub-system can be designed and tested independently. On a block diagram, sub-systems are shown as blocks that can themselves be expanded into more detailed block diagrams &mdash; this is called hierarchical decomposition.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">The system boundary decides what counts as an input and what counts as an output. For a car engine management sub-system, &ldquo;throttle position&rdquo; is an input (from outside the sub-system, controlled by the driver), whereas &ldquo;fuel injector pulse width&rdquo; is an output (leaving the sub-system to control the fuel delivery).</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Schematic Drawings</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A diagram that uses standard symbols to show how components in a system (electrical, electronic or hydraulic) are connected, without showing their physical layout.">schematic drawing</dfn> (or schematic) uses standard symbols to show how components connect, not how they are physically arranged on a PCB or in a machine. Electrical schematics use British Standard (BS 3939) symbols: zigzag for a resistor, two parallel lines for a capacitor, a circle with an arrow for a motor. Hydraulic and pneumatic schematics use ISO 1219 symbols: rectangles for cylinders, diamonds for filters, arrows on lines for flow direction.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Schematics are the standard language of engineering workshops. If you hand a schematic to a technician, they can identify every component and trace every connection regardless of what the physical product looks like. This is why drawing interpretation is a key skill examined in the written paper.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Flowcharts: Four Standard Symbols</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Flowcharts show the sequence of steps in a process or control program. There are four standard symbols you must know:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Terminator (oval)</strong> &mdash; marks the START or END of the process. Every flowchart must begin and end with a terminator.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Process box (rectangle)</strong> &mdash; shows an action to be carried out, e.g. &ldquo;Switch motor ON&rdquo; or &ldquo;Heat element for 30 s&rdquo;.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Input/Output parallelogram</strong> &mdash; shows data entering or leaving the system, e.g. &ldquo;Read sensor value&rdquo; or &ldquo;Display temperature&rdquo;.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Decision diamond</strong> &mdash; asks a YES/NO question, e.g. &ldquo;Is temperature &gt; 80&deg;C?&rdquo; with two arrow paths leaving it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p data-narration-id="n24">Feedback loops in flowcharts must loop back to a decision diamond or an earlier process box &mdash; <em>never</em> to the start terminator. A common error is drawing the feedback arrow going all the way back to START, creating an infinite loop with no way to exit.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n25">Choosing the Right Representation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n26">The examiner expects you to choose the correct type of diagram for the question. Use a <strong>block diagram</strong> when asked to show how sub-systems relate and where signals flow. Use a <strong>schematic</strong> when asked to show component connections using standard symbols. Use a <strong>flowchart</strong> when asked to show the sequence of steps or decisions in a process or control program. Mixing these up is one of the most common reasons students lose marks on systems questions.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n28">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">The input&ndash;process&ndash;output (IPO) model breaks any system into what enters it, what it does, and what it produces &mdash; closed-loop systems add a feedback path that corrects the output.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">System block diagrams show sub-systems as labelled rectangles with arrows showing signal or energy flow &mdash; they are not circuit diagrams and not flowcharts.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31">Flowcharts use four standard symbols (terminator, process, input/output, decision) to show the sequence of steps; schematics use standard component symbols to show how parts are connected.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n27">If a question asks you to &ldquo;draw a system block diagram&rdquo;, use labelled rectangles and arrows only &mdash; no component symbols. If it asks you to &ldquo;complete the flowchart&rdquo;, check you have used the correct shapes: oval for start/end, rectangle for process, diamond for decisions. Feedback loops must point back to a decision or process box, never straight to START. For a 5-mark flowchart question, count your boxes before submitting &mdash; one missing step is typically one mark dropped.</p>

