=== L2 Mechanical Systems: Motion, Linkages and Cams ===
id: 0d1fd083-58d9-445e-84c4-1cb8e4c1ec98
slug: mechanical-systems-motion-linkages-and-cams
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:21:30.530485+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Types of motion, linkages, conversion mechanisms and cam profiles explained clearly.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Four Types of Motion</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Mechanical systems transmit and transform movement. Before designing a mechanism you must identify what type of motion is needed at the output. There are four types that engineers work with every day.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3"><dfn class="term" data-def="Continuous circular movement around a fixed central axis, such as a rotating shaft or wheel.">Rotary motion</dfn> is spinning around a fixed axis &mdash; a motor shaft, a gear, a wheel. <dfn class="term" data-def="Movement in a straight line from one point to another.">Linear motion</dfn> is movement in a straight line, such as the travel of a drill bit moving into a workpiece. <dfn class="term" data-def="Back-and-forth movement along a straight line, such as a piston in a cylinder.">Reciprocating motion</dfn> is back-and-forth movement along a straight line &mdash; a piston, a saw blade or a needle in an industrial sewing machine. <dfn class="term" data-def="Swinging or rocking movement about a central point, like a pendulum or a windscreen wiper.">Oscillating motion</dfn> is a swinging or rocking motion around a fixed point, like a pendulum or a windscreen wiper arm.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n4">In the examination you must use the correct technical terms &mdash; answers like &ldquo;goes round and round&rdquo; or &ldquo;goes sideways&rdquo; are not credited. A motor produces <em>rotary</em> motion; a piston produces <em>reciprocating</em> motion.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name all four motion types, then write one real machine example for each from memory.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four types of motion are rotary (spinning around an axis), linear (straight line), reciprocating (back-and-forth straight line) and oscillating (swinging around a fixed point). Most mechanisms convert one type of motion into another.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Linkages and Conversion of Motion</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A mechanism made of rigid bars (links) connected by pivots that transmits or transforms motion and force.">linkage</dfn> is a set of rigid bars joined by pivots that transmits or changes the direction of movement. Engineers use linkages when they need to transmit force around corners, reverse direction, or change the shape of a motion path.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The simplest linkage is a <strong>reverse linkage</strong> (bell crank). Pushing one arm down causes the other arm to push outward &mdash; the motion direction changes by 90&deg;. Bicycle brake levers use this principle: squeezing the handle pulls a cable that pushes brake pads against the wheel rim. A <strong>parallel linkage</strong> keeps two bars moving in the same direction &mdash; used in toy train pantographs and car suspension systems.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Converting Rotary to Reciprocating: Cranks and Sliders</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A mechanism that converts rotary motion into reciprocating linear motion, consisting of a rotating crank connected to a sliding element by a connecting rod.">crank and slider mechanism</dfn> is the fundamental converter of rotary motion into reciprocating motion. A rotating crank pin is connected to a slider (piston) by a connecting rod. As the crank rotates, the slider moves back and forth. Every internal combustion engine uses this principle &mdash; the rotating crankshaft drives the pistons up and down.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The reverse is also possible: a reciprocating steam engine drives a piston back and forth, which turns a crankshaft via a connecting rod to produce rotary motion at the wheels. Engineers describe this as converting <em>reciprocating to rotary</em> motion.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Cams and Followers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A shaped plate or disc that rotates and pushes a follower up and down as its profile (outline) changes.">cam</dfn> is a specially shaped piece that rotates and pushes a component called a <dfn class="term" data-def="A component that rests against a cam and moves up and down as the cam rotates, following the cam's profile.">follower</dfn> up and down as it turns. The key principle: <strong>the position of the follower at any instant depends entirely on the radius of the cam at the point of contact</strong>. Where the cam radius is large, the follower is pushed up. Where the radius is small, the follower drops down. This is the examiner&rsquo;s most-tested cam concept &mdash; you must be able to explain it, not just name it.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in one sentence why a follower moves up and down as a cam rotates — mention cam profile and radius in your answer.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The follower&rsquo;s position at any moment is determined by the cam profile &mdash; specifically the radius of the cam at the point of contact. A large radius pushes the follower high; a small radius lets it drop. The cam profile is therefore what engineers adjust to control the follower&rsquo;s movement pattern.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Named Cam Profiles</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Four cam profiles appear in engineering exam questions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n15"><strong>Pear cam</strong> &mdash; egg-shaped profile. The follower rises slowly to a maximum height and then drops quickly. Used in car engine valve timing, where a valve must open slowly and snap shut.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n16"><strong>Eccentric (circular) cam</strong> &mdash; a circular disc with the pivot offset from the centre. As it rotates the follower rises and falls smoothly in a simple harmonic pattern. Used in pumps and simple oscillating machines.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Snail (drop) cam</strong> &mdash; the follower rises very gradually around most of the rotation, then drops almost vertically as it reaches the &ldquo;snail&rsquo;s tail&rdquo; drop. Used in clock escapements and stamping machines where a sudden downward force is needed.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Heart cam</strong> &mdash; heart-shaped profile that converts the continuous rotation into a back-and-forth motion of equal rate in both directions. Used in mechanisms where a constant-rate reciprocation is needed.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Choosing the Right Mechanism</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">When an engineer needs to convert motion, they select the mechanism based on the motion required and the forces involved. To convert rotary to reciprocating: use a cam or a crank and slider. To change the direction of a force: use a linkage (bell crank, reverse linkage). To transmit rotary motion between shafts that are far apart or at an angle: use gears, chains or belts (covered in Lesson 3).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21">At Rolls-Royce Turbine Assemblies in Derby, precision cams in the engine-control unit govern the opening and closing of fuel valves with timings accurate to thousandths of a second. The cam profile is machined to tolerances of &plusmn;0.005&thinsp;mm because any deviation changes the valve timing and affects thrust output. This real-world context shows why understanding cam profiles matters beyond the classroom.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The four types of motion are rotary (spinning), linear (straight line), reciprocating (back-and-forth along a line) and oscillating (swinging around a pivot) &mdash; you must use the correct technical names in exam answers.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Linkages use rigid bars and pivots to transmit or redirect force; the crank-and-slider mechanism converts rotary motion to reciprocating motion (and vice versa).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">A cam profile determines the follower&rsquo;s movement &mdash; the follower rises when the cam radius is large and drops when it is small; the four named profiles (pear, eccentric, snail, heart) each produce a different motion pattern.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">If asked to &ldquo;explain how a cam produces movement in the follower&rdquo;, use the phrase <em>cam profile</em> and link it to the radius: &ldquo;As the cam rotates, the radius at the contact point changes. Where the radius is greater, the follower is pushed further up; where the radius is smaller, the follower drops down.&rdquo; Simply naming the cam type without explaining the radius principle will not earn full marks. Also remember: exam questions use the technical names &mdash; write <em>reciprocating</em> not &ldquo;back and forth&rdquo; and <em>rotary</em> not &ldquo;round and round&rdquo;.</p>

