=== L3 Mechanical Calculations: Gears, Pulleys and Mechanical Advantage ===
id: 2dc4c9ef-040a-4a6f-9768-ef0b170a4926
slug: mechanical-calculations-gears-pulleys-and-mechanical-advantage
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:26:40.54204+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 29

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Gear ratios, pulley systems and mechanical advantage calculations with worked examples.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Use Gears and Pulleys?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Gears and pulleys are power-transmission components &mdash; they carry force and motion from one shaft or point to another. They are also <em>force multipliers</em>: by choosing the right gear ratio or pulley arrangement, engineers can increase or reduce force, speed and torque to match what the application needs. A bicycle has a small front chainring and a large rear sprocket in low gear so the rider can pedal easily uphill; in high gear the ratio reverses to allow high speed on a flat road.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Gear Trains and Gear Ratio</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="Two or more meshing gears that transmit rotary motion and can change speed, torque or direction of rotation.">gear train</dfn> consists of two or more gears with interlocking teeth. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The gear connected to the power source (e.g. motor), which transmits motion to the driven gear.">driver gear</dfn> is connected to the motor or input shaft. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The gear that receives motion from the driver gear; it is the output of the gear pair.">driven gear</dfn> is connected to the output shaft. When the driver turns, its teeth push the driven gear&rsquo;s teeth in the opposite direction.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The ratio of the number of teeth on the driven gear to the number of teeth on the driver gear, indicating how many times the driver must rotate to turn the driven gear once.">gear ratio</dfn> tells you how many times the driver must rotate to turn the driven gear once:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">
$$\text{Gear ratio} = \frac{\text{teeth on driven gear}}{\text{teeth on driver gear}}$$
</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Example: a driver gear has 20 teeth and a driven gear has 60 teeth.
$$\text{Gear ratio} = \frac{60}{20} = 3:1$$
This means the driver must rotate 3 times for the driven gear to complete one full turn. The driven gear turns at one-third of the speed but with three times the torque. This is a <strong>speed reducer</strong> &mdash; used when high torque is needed at low speed (e.g. a winch, a robot arm).</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n8" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the gear ratio formula from memory, then calculate the ratio for a 15-tooth driver and a 45-tooth driven gear.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Gear ratio = driven teeth &divide; driver teeth. A ratio greater than 1 (more teeth on driven than driver) means the output turns slower but with greater torque. A ratio less than 1 means the output turns faster but with less torque. Always state which gear is the driver in your working.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Compound Gear Trains, Chains and Sprockets</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A gear train in which one or more shafts carry two gears, allowing large overall gear ratios in a compact space.">compound gear train</dfn> uses intermediate shafts that each carry two gears. This lets engineers achieve very large overall gear ratios without making gears unreasonably large. A clock uses a compound gear train to step down the motor speed to one revolution per minute at the minute hand.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A chain-and-sprocket drive that transmits rotary motion between two parallel shafts; the chain engages the teeth of both sprockets.">chain and sprocket</dfn> drive works on the same principle as a gear train but the two sprockets can be far apart. The chain transmits force without slipping (unlike a belt drive). Bicycles, motorcycles and industrial conveyors use chain-and-sprocket drives. Gear ratio is still calculated using tooth counts: driven teeth &divide; driver teeth.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Pulleys and Velocity Ratio</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A wheel with a grooved rim over which a rope or belt runs, used to change the direction of a force or to transmit power between shafts.">pulley</dfn> changes the direction of a force or transmits rotary motion between shafts using a belt. A single fixed pulley only changes direction &mdash; you pull down on the rope and the load goes up, but you still need a force equal to the load&rsquo;s weight. A block and tackle uses multiple pulleys to reduce the effort needed.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">For a pulley system lifting a load, the <dfn class="term" data-def="The ratio of the distance moved by the effort to the distance moved by the load; equal to the number of rope sections supporting the load in a block-and-tackle system.">velocity ratio</dfn> (VR) equals the number of rope sections supporting the movable block:
$$\text{Velocity ratio} = \text{number of rope sections supporting the load}$$
With 4 rope sections, VR = 4: the effort moves 4 metres for every 1 metre the load rises. This also means the effort force needed is approximately one-quarter of the load (ignoring friction).</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Mechanical Advantage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15"><dfn class="term" data-def="The ratio of the load (output force) to the effort (input force) in a mechanism; a measure of how much a machine multiplies force.">Mechanical advantage</dfn> (MA) tells you how much a mechanism multiplies the input force:
$$\text{MA} = \frac{\text{load}}{\text{effort}}$$
If a pulley system requires an effort of 200&thinsp;N to lift a load of 800&thinsp;N:
$$\text{MA} = \frac{800}{200} = 4$$
An MA of 4 means the machine multiplies the input force by 4. A higher MA means less effort is needed, but the effort must be applied over a greater distance. Real machines always have an MA slightly less than the VR because friction wastes some energy.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close this and write the mechanical advantage formula from memory, then calculate MA when a 50 N effort lifts a 300 N load.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Mechanical advantage (MA) = load &divide; effort. An MA greater than 1 means the machine multiplies your force &mdash; it takes less effort to move a large load. Always include units: load and effort are both in newtons (N). MA itself has no units because it is a ratio.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Bearings and Friction Reduction</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A component that supports a rotating or sliding shaft and reduces friction between moving parts.">bearing</dfn> supports a rotating shaft and reduces friction. The two main types you need to know are <strong>plain (journal) bearings</strong> and <strong>ball (rolling) bearings</strong>. A plain bearing is a sleeve of soft metal or polymer that the shaft slides against; it relies on an oil film. A ball bearing uses small steel balls running in a hardened steel race &mdash; the rolling contact dramatically reduces friction compared with sliding contact.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18">In the examination, &ldquo;spins&rdquo; alone is insufficient to describe what a ball bearing does. You must say that it <em>reduces friction between rotating parts</em>, allowing the shaft to turn with less energy loss and less heat generation. Ball bearings are used in electric motors, wheel hubs, gearboxes and any mechanism where a shaft must rotate at speed for extended periods.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Worked Calculation: Gear Ratio</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">Bramford Drives Ltd makes conveyor gearboxes for a bottling plant. The motor pinion has 18 teeth; the output gear has 72 teeth. Calculate the gear ratio.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21">
$$\text{Gear ratio} = \frac{\text{teeth on driven}}{\text{teeth on driver}} = \frac{72}{18} = 4:1$$
The output shaft turns at one-quarter of the motor speed. If the motor runs at 1440&thinsp;rpm, the conveyor shaft turns at 360&thinsp;rpm with four times the torque.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n22">Worked Calculation: Mechanical Advantage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n23">A technician uses a block-and-tackle with 3 rope sections to lift a 600&thinsp;N engine component. What effort is required (assume ideal conditions)?</p>
<p data-narration-id="n24">Velocity ratio = 3 (three rope sections). In ideal conditions MA = VR = 3.
$$\text{Effort} = \frac{\text{load}}{\text{MA}} = \frac{600}{3} = 200\text{ N}$$
In practice, friction in the pulleys means the actual effort needed would be higher than 200&thinsp;N, giving a real MA slightly less than 3.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n26">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Gear ratio = teeth on driven gear &divide; teeth on driver gear. A ratio above 1 reduces output speed and increases torque; a ratio below 1 increases speed and reduces torque.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">Mechanical advantage (MA) = load &divide; effort. An MA greater than 1 means the machine multiplies your input force; in a pulley system, MA equals the number of rope sections supporting the load (in ideal conditions).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Ball bearings reduce friction between rotating parts by replacing sliding contact with rolling contact; always state this function explicitly in exam answers rather than just writing &ldquo;spins&rdquo;.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n25">For calculation questions, always show your formula first, then substitute values with units, then give the final answer. Method marks are awarded even if your final answer is wrong. For gear ratio questions, check which gear is the driver and which is the driven &mdash; using them the wrong way round gives the reciprocal of the correct answer. For mechanical advantage, check units: load and effort must both be in newtons. MA itself has no unit. If a question asks you to &ldquo;give two reasons for using a pulley&rdquo;, name two distinct benefits: reduces the force needed AND changes the direction of effort &mdash; these count as separate reasons.</p>

