=== L6 Programmable Systems: Microcontrollers and Interfacing ===
id: f9c783a2-6729-4b1e-ac26-515a05027bd8
slug: programmable-systems-microcontrollers-and-interfacing
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:57:33.267585+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 34

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How microcontrollers work, flowchart programming, ADC and interfacing with transistors.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Microcontroller?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A small integrated circuit that contains a processor, memory and programmable input and output connections on a single chip, used to control engineered systems.">microcontroller</dfn> is a small computer on a single chip. It contains a processor (to execute instructions), memory (to store the program and data) and a set of input and output connections &mdash; all on one integrated circuit. Microcontrollers run a program that reads sensor inputs, makes decisions and drives output devices. They replace large banks of discrete logic gates, making control systems compact, flexible and reprogrammable.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Common microcontroller families include the <strong>PIC</strong> (Peripheral Interface Controller &mdash; widely used in industrial and automotive applications) and development boards such as the <strong>BBC micro:bit</strong> and <strong>Arduino</strong> (used in school workshops and rapid prototyping). For the purposes of this specification, the key rule is that a microcontroller is limited to <strong>three inputs and three outputs</strong> within an engineered system &mdash; this keeps the design manageable at GCSE level.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the input-process-output model for a microcontroller system from memory, then give one real example for each stage.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The microcontroller follows the input&ndash;process&ndash;output model. Sensors and switches are inputs; the microcontroller&rsquo;s program is the process; actuators (motors, solenoids, LEDs, buzzers) are outputs. The program runs in a continuous loop: read inputs &rarr; make decision &rarr; set outputs &rarr; repeat.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Flowchart Programming</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Microcontrollers are typically programmed using a <dfn class="term" data-def="A visual programming method that represents the steps of a program as a sequence of standard flowchart symbols (terminator, process, input/output, decision), which can be directly translated into code.">flowchart program</dfn> at GCSE level. A flowchart shows exactly what the microcontroller does at each step, making it easy to trace the logic before writing code. The standard symbols are the same four shapes from Lesson 1: oval (start/stop), rectangle (process), parallelogram (input/output) and diamond (decision).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">A typical microcontroller flowchart for a temperature-controlled fan looks like this in sequence:</p>
<ol>
  <li data-narration-id="n8">START</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n9">Read temperature sensor (ADC converts analogue output to digital number)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n10">Decision: is temperature above threshold (e.g. 30&deg;C)?</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n11">YES &rarr; Switch fan motor ON; loop back to &ldquo;Read temperature sensor&rdquo;</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n12">NO &rarr; Switch fan motor OFF; loop back to &ldquo;Read temperature sensor&rdquo;</li>
</ol>
<p data-narration-id="n13">This loop runs continuously while the system is powered. The decision diamond and its YES/NO branches create the responsive behaviour. Changing the threshold value in the program immediately changes the system behaviour &mdash; no rewiring needed. This is a key advantage of programmable systems over fixed logic-gate circuits.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why Use a Microcontroller Instead of Discrete Logic Gates?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Discrete logic gates are fixed &mdash; once a circuit is built, its behaviour is determined by the physical wiring. Adding a new condition means redesigning and rebuilding the circuit. A microcontroller replaces all the logic gates with a program, so:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n15">Behaviour can be changed by reprogramming &mdash; no hardware changes needed.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n16">Multiple conditions and complex decision trees can be handled by a single chip that would require dozens of logic gates if built from discrete components.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n17">The same hardware platform (e.g. Arduino) can be programmed to control a fan, a door lock or a conveyor &mdash; reducing component variety and manufacturing cost.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n18">Timing functions (delays, PWM motor speed control) are built into the microcontroller &mdash; they would require separate timer ICs in a discrete circuit.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">The ADC and Sensor Thresholds</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">Most real-world sensors (thermistors, LDRs, pressure transducers) produce <dfn class="term" data-def="A continuously varying voltage output from a sensor that represents a physical quantity; must be converted to digital before a microcontroller can use it.">analogue sensor outputs</dfn>. The microcontroller&rsquo;s internal ADC converts these to a digital number (typically 0&ndash;1023 for a 10-bit ADC). The program then compares this number to a <dfn class="term" data-def="A preset digital value in the microcontroller program that the sensor reading is compared against to trigger an action.">sensor threshold</dfn>: if the reading exceeds the threshold, the output is triggered; if not, it stays off.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21">Example: an LDR connected to a 10-bit ADC produces values from 0 (very dark) to 1023 (very bright). The program might set a threshold of 200: if reading &lt; 200, switch on the lamp; if reading &ge; 200, switch off. Adjusting the threshold changes the light level at which the lamp triggers, without any soldering.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n22" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in two sentences why a threshold value is used in a microcontroller program, mentioning ADC and sensor reading.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A sensor threshold is a number stored in the microcontroller program. The ADC converts the analogue sensor voltage into a digital value; the program compares this to the threshold and decides what action to take. Changing the threshold changes the system&rsquo;s sensitivity without rewiring &mdash; this is a major advantage of programmable control over fixed logic.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Interfacing: Transistors and FETs as Driver Components</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n23">Microcontroller output pins supply only a small current (typically 20&ndash;40&thinsp;mA). Most actuators (motors, solenoids, lamps) draw far more current than this. Connecting a motor directly to an output pin would damage or destroy the microcontroller. An <dfn class="term" data-def="A component placed between a microcontroller output and a high-current load device to allow the low-current control signal to switch the larger load current safely.">interfacing component</dfn> is used to switch the larger current safely.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n24">The two main interfacing components are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Bipolar junction transistor (BJT)</strong> &mdash; a small base current from the microcontroller controls a much larger collector-to-emitter current through the load. Think of it as a current-controlled switch. An NPN transistor is used with the load between the positive supply and the collector, and the emitter to ground.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Field-effect transistor (FET/MOSFET)</strong> &mdash; a voltage on the gate terminal switches a much larger current through the drain-to-source path. FETs are voltage-controlled (not current-controlled like BJTs) and are preferred for driving motors and high-power LEDs because they can switch rapidly and waste very little energy when fully on.</li>
    </ul>
    <p data-narration-id="n27">A protection diode (flyback diode) is placed across an inductive load (motor, solenoid) in parallel to absorb the voltage spike that occurs when the device is switched off. Without it, the spike can destroy the transistor or FET.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n28">Industrial Application: Pick-and-Place Machines</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n29">A pick-and-place machine on an electronics production line uses a microcontroller to control three outputs: a vacuum gripper (picks up components), an X-Y positioning motor (moves to target location) and a placement actuator (releases the component). Three sensors (position encoder, component-present sensor, placement-complete sensor) provide the three inputs. The microcontroller loops through its flowchart program hundreds of times per second, precisely coordinating all six connections. This is exactly the three-inputs, three-outputs model described in the specification.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n31">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n32">A microcontroller contains a processor, memory and I/O on one chip; it reads sensor inputs (via ADC if analogue), runs a flowchart-based program and drives output actuators &mdash; the three-inputs-three-outputs model.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n33">Flowchart programs use four symbols (oval, rectangle, parallelogram, diamond); the decision diamond creates branching and looping behaviour that replaces banks of discrete logic gates, and can be updated by reprogramming without hardware changes.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n34">Interfacing components (transistors and FETs) are needed between microcontroller output pins and high-current actuators; flyback diodes protect against inductive voltage spikes when motors or solenoids switch off.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n30">If a question asks you to &ldquo;explain why a microcontroller is used instead of discrete logic gates&rdquo;, focus on two advantages: (1) reprogrammable &mdash; behaviour can be changed without rewiring; (2) handles complex multiple conditions in a compact single chip. For flowchart questions, always use the four correct symbols &mdash; draw the diamond for decisions, the rectangle for processes. A common error is putting a decision in a rectangle &mdash; this loses a mark. If a question mentions ADC, always explain that it converts an analogue sensor voltage to a digital number the microcontroller can compare to a threshold.</p>

